\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Structural comparison between the traditional pivot translation approach (routing through Standard Bangla) and the proposed direct poly-dialectal mapping topology.}}{7}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces End-to-end architectural workflow of the Poly-Dialectal Neural Machine Translation Framework, depicting five phases: source corpora integration from seven existing datasets, the data processing pipeline, NMT model fine-tuning with LoRA, multi-directional translation paradigm, and evaluation with four standard MT metrics.}}{7}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Evolution of Bangla regional dialect datasets and resources over time, highlighting the scale and dialectal coverage of the proposed corpus.}}{11}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the proposed multi-dialectal parallel corpus size against previously established datasets.}}{11}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Dataset Composition and Source Provenance: A stacked horizontal distribution illustrating the severe class imbalance alongside the precise proportional contributions from prior corpora and manual augmentation efforts for each regional dialect.}}{17}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Cross-Dialect Parallel Coverage Matrix: An upper-triangular masked heatmap detailing the exact density of shared, parallel sentence pairs intersecting any two evaluated dialectal variants.}}{17}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Parameter efficiency of LoRA fine-tuning across the three evaluated model architectures, demonstrating over 98\% reduction in trainable parameters. Furthermore, we extend our methodology by introducing \textbf {Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation (DoRA)} \cite {liu2024dora}, which decomposes the pre-trained weights into magnitude and directional components for fine-tuning. This approach maintains the parameter efficiency of LoRA while achieving learning capacity closer to full fine-tuning. A visual comparison distinguishing LoRA's structure from DoRA's decomposed optimization is provided in Figure~\ref {fig:lora_efficiency}.}}{21}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Comparative evaluation across experimental phases. BLEU \cite {papineni2002bleu}, chrF++ \cite {popovic2015chrf}, and METEOR \cite {banerjee2005meteor} (higher is better) and TER \cite {snover2006study} (lower is better) are shown for all three models at 20 epochs and BanglaT5 \cite {bhattacharjee-etal-2023-banglanlg} at 100 epochs.}}{24}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Cross-dialectal BLEU \cite {papineni2002bleu} score heatmap (BanglaT5 \cite {bhattacharjee-etal-2023-banglanlg}, 100 epochs). Darker shading indicates higher translation quality. Low-resource dialects (Rajshahi, Rangpur) exhibit uniformly lower scores, while morphologically proximate pairs (Mymensingh$\leftrightarrow $SCB) achieve the highest scores.}}{25}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Scatter plot illustrating the inverse relationship between a dialect's linguistic proximity to Standard Bangla and the resulting translation quality (BLEU score).}}{26}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Translation directionality asymmetry across six major dialects. Dialect$\rightarrow $SCB (green) consistently outperforms SCB$\rightarrow $Dialect (blue), with the BLEU \cite {papineni2002bleu} gap indicated by annotations.}}{26}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Distribution of translation errors across five linguistic failure categories, derived from manual analysis of 200 high-TER \cite {snover2006study} failure cases.}}{27}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces BLEU \cite {papineni2002bleu} and chrF++ \cite {popovic2015chrf} as a function of training data volume for two LoRA \cite {hu2021lora} configurations. Consistent improvement is observed, with diminishing returns beyond approximately 3,000 samples.}}{28}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Visual comparison of BLEU \cite {papineni2002bleu} and chrF++ \cite {popovic2015chrf} scores across baseline systems and the proposed approach.}}{29}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Multidimensional performance trade-off (Radar chart) demonstrating the advantages of INT8 quantization in reducing latency and memory footprint while preserving translation quality.}}{32}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The deployed Streamlit web application interface showing source/target dialect selectors, text input area, and translation output panel.}}{32}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Standard Bangla to Sylheti translation demonstration, showing successful morphological and lexical adaptation.}}{33}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Direct dialect-to-dialect translation from Chittagong to Noakhali, bypassing the Standard Bangla pivot.}}{34}{figure.caption.29}%
